#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084524 :tutorial/content "To determine the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=ax^2+bx+c,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{b}{2a}\n$$\n\nWe'll derive this result in a separate lesson. For now, let's get some practice working with it. \n\nSuppose we want to find the axis of symmetry of the function $y=3x^2-6x+1.$ To do this, we substitute $a=3$ and $b=-6$ into the above formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{b}{2a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(-6)}{2(3)} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is the equation of the axis of symmetry. \n\n![](images/88/88ee917eb387ee60e67080785519ffe9cb4333adcd602a733659d0447c37f61a.png)\n\nThe vertex of the parabola lies on the axis of symmetry, so $x=1$ also represents the $x$-coordinate of the vertex. To find the $y$-coordinate of the vertex, we can substitute $x=1$ into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3{x}^{2}-6x+1 \\\\  & =3(1)^{2}-6(1)+1 \\\\  & =3-6+1 \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is at $(1,-2).$\n\n![](images/a3/a391338ffb2bddd2a58b836e328c574d348215c518b27d9886ca11a21674943a.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "bfd0e6a4-644a-50b6-91df-852343ebd41e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9241 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
